  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Given the line \\(3x+5y=15\\) and a point on this line equidistant from the coordinate axes. Such a point exists in:",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "none of the quadrants"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "quadrant \\(\\mathrm{I}\\) only"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "quadrants \\(\\mathrm{I},\\) \\(\\mathrm{II}\\)\nonly"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "quadrants \\(\\mathrm{I},\\) \\(\\mathrm{II},\\) \\(\\mathrm{III}\\)\nonly"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "each of the quadrants"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "c",
    "value": null
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.ahsme-1960-p30.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "For \\(y=x,\\) the line gives \\(8x=15,\\) producing a point in quadrant I. For \\(y=-x,\\) it gives \\(-2x=15,\\) so \\(x\\lt0\\) and \\(y>0,\\) producing a point in quadrant II. There are no other possibilities.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C.",
      "hints": [
        "A point equidistant from the axes satisfies \\(|x|=|y|\\)",
        "Intersect the given line with both \\(y=x\\) and \\(y=-x\\)"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
